  "Number": 24,
  "Question": "The function \\(4x^2-12x-1:\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "always increases as \\(x\\) increases",
    "B": "always decreases as \\(x\\) decreases to \\(1\\)",
    "C": "cannot equal \\(0\\)",
    "D": "has a maximum value when \\(x\\) is negative",
    "E": "has a minimum value of \\(-10\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "To find a minimum, rewrite the expression as a square plus a constant. Since\n\\[\n(2x-3)^2=4x^2-12x+9,\n\\]\nwe have\n\\[\n4x^2-12x-1=(2x-3)^2-10.\n\\]\nFor every real \\(x\\), the square is nonnegative, so the function is at least \\(-10\\). This value is attained when \\(2x-3=0\\), or \\(x=\\tfrac32\\). Hence its minimum value is \\(\\boxed{-10}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
